Make hermetic-llvm's Linux system-header directories remote-execution-safe.

hermetic-llvm hands the Linux kernel + glibc system headers to compile actions
through TWO independent paths, and both inject a bare source-directory artifact
(one opaque directory node) rather than the individual header files:

  Path A -- cc_args(data = [..._directory]) in toolchain/args/linux: feeds the
  kernel/glibc headers to ordinary "complete" (target-config) compiles.

  Path B -- the kernel_headers / gnu_libc_headers cc_library targets
  (hdrs = ["include"]), consumed via @llvm-project's libcxx/libcxxabi/libunwind
  implementation_deps. This is the ONLY header source for the exec-config
  "stage0" runtime compiles, which receive no kernel-header cc_args at all.

Local sandboxed builds follow the directory symlink fine, but remote execution
must expand a source directory into each action's Merkle input tree; the
include/ subtree (e.g. include/linux/) is not enumerated and gets dropped,
giving "fatal error: 'linux/limits.h' file not found" on the exec-config
toolchain-bootstrap [for tool] compiles. It is deterministic once the action
cache is cold; warm action-cache hits skip the compile and mask the gap, so it
presents as flaky. A Bazel-side input-declaration issue, not a backend bug.

Two coupled changes:

1. directory.bzl gains an opt-in expand_files arg (set for the kernel + glibc
   header repos) that makes DefaultInfo carry the individual header files (a
   recursive glob, minus `make headers_install` byproducts) instead of one
   source-directory artifact, so the Merkle-tree builder enumerates every header.
   This fixes Path A. The macOS SDK sysroot also uses headers_directory but is
   left unexpanded -- expanding it would bloat every darwin *local* compile.
   The toolchain module map needs no change: runtimes/module_map.bzl's
   _include_path_impl reads src[DirectoryInfo].transitive_files for every
   DirectoryInfo source and never its DefaultInfo, so a file-list DefaultInfo
   leaves the generated module map as it was.

2. The kernel_headers / gnu_libc_headers cc_library targets change
   hdrs = ["include"] (one source-directory artifact) to
   hdrs = glob(["include/**"]), so every individual header becomes a declared
   action input for the exec-config stage0 runtime compiles. This fixes Path B --
   the path the expand_files change above does not reach.

Drop if/when hermetic-llvm ships an equivalent fix upstream
(https://github.com/hermeticbuild/hermetic-llvm).

diff --git a/directory.bzl b/directory.bzl
--- a/directory.bzl
+++ b/directory.bzl
@@ -5,7 +5,13 @@
 # We want to put a source directory into the DefaultInfo but still propagate
 # the DirectoryInfo for header inclusion checking and the toolchain's module
 # map.
-def headers_directory(name, path, visibility = None):
+#
+# expand_files: when True, DefaultInfo carries the individual header files
+# (a recursive glob) instead of a single source-directory artifact. Remote
+# execution must expand a source directory into the action's Merkle input tree
+# and drops its subtree; enumerating discrete File artifacts makes the input
+# tree complete.
+def headers_directory(name, path, visibility = None, expand_files = False):
     if path == ".":
         directory(
             name = name + "_directory",
@@ -25,7 +31,13 @@
 
     native.filegroup(
         name = name + "_source_directory",
-        srcs = [path],
+        srcs = native.glob(
+            ["**"] if path == "." else [path + "/**"],
+            # Drop `make headers_install` byproducts; they are not headers and
+            # would otherwise enter the action input set as upload-only noise.
+            exclude = ["**/.install", "**/..install.cmd", "**/.*.cmd"],
+            allow_empty = True,
+        ) if expand_files else [path],
     )
 
     _headers_directory(
diff --git a/kernel/extension/kernel-headers.BUILD.bazel b/kernel/extension/kernel-headers.BUILD.bazel
--- a/kernel/extension/kernel-headers.BUILD.bazel
+++ b/kernel/extension/kernel-headers.BUILD.bazel
@@ -3,7 +3,10 @@
 
 cc_library(
     name = "kernel_headers",
-    hdrs = ["include"],
+    hdrs = glob(
+        ["include/**"],
+        exclude = ["**/.install", "**/..install.cmd", "**/.*.cmd"],
+    ),
     # Any code should always get passed linux UAPI headers as -isystem
     features = ["system_include_paths"],
     includes = ["include"],
@@ -13,5 +16,6 @@
 headers_directory(
     name = "kernel_headers_directory",
     path = "include",
+    expand_files = True,
     visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
 )
diff --git a/runtimes/glibc/extension/glibc-headers.BUILD.bazel b/runtimes/glibc/extension/glibc-headers.BUILD.bazel
--- a/runtimes/glibc/extension/glibc-headers.BUILD.bazel
+++ b/runtimes/glibc/extension/glibc-headers.BUILD.bazel
@@ -3,7 +3,10 @@
 
 cc_library(
     name = "gnu_libc_headers",
-    hdrs = ["include"],
+    hdrs = glob(
+        ["include/**"],
+        exclude = ["**/.install", "**/..install.cmd", "**/.*.cmd"],
+    ),
     # user code should always get glibc headers as -isystem
     # but it seems glibc itself includes as <...>
     features = ["system_include_paths"],
@@ -14,5 +17,6 @@
 headers_directory(
     name = "glibc_headers_directory",
     path = "include",
+    expand_files = True,
     visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
 )
